Titles & Modes with “Frontline”
Frontline (on Steam)


This is advertised as a squad-based shooter where you join a group of up to 5 “Warriors” in a war against a tyrant named Nihilus. Steam Store+1


Game modes: It has both a Story mode (campaign) and a Multiplayer mode they call “Endless War.” Steam Store+1


In Multiplayer: matches evolve in phases — from solo looting to team-based objectives — then culminating in an “Oblivion Event.” Steam Store


In the tactical phase, teams (4v4 or 5v5) fight where one side must plant an “Oblivion device,” and the other must prevent activation. Steam Store+1


When device triggers, battlefield changes: environmental dangers emerge, forcing new strategies. Steam Store


Status: The Steam page describes the game as not yet released (or still in development?). Steam Store+1

Frontlines: Fuel of War (2008 FPS)
If by “Frontline Multiplayer” you meant older or classic FPS — this might be it. Wikipedia+1


It’s a first-person shooter, with both single-player campaign and multiplayer modes. Wikipedia+1


The central idea is the “frontline” — a shifting zone of conflict that forces teams to fight over control. As the frontline moves, control, equipment availability, and tactical goals shift. Wikipedia+1


Matches support large-scale combat: many players, vehicles, and tactical roles (e.g. assault, support, drones, etc.). GamerNode+1


Important mechanic: a UAV drone system — drone reveals enemies on HUD/map, can scout or act as kamikaze. Wikipedia


Note: online PC servers were shut down around 2011 — only LAN or legacy multiplayer remains. Wikipedia



“Frontline” as a Game Mode (in other games)
Sometimes “Frontline” doesn’t refer to a full game — but a multiplayer mode featured in games. For example:


In World of Tanks, there’s a “Frontline” mode: 30 vs 30 battles with tier-restricted vehicles (Tier VIII), special battle rules, respawns, capture zones, and strategic objectives. WoT Asia — Free Online Tanks Game+1


In some shooters (e.g. Call of Duty: Mobile), “Frontline” mode works like a respawn-based team deathmatch: players spawn at base, kill enemies, and reach a kill/score limit to win. Call of Duty Wiki+1



✅ Why the confusion & What’s Important


Because many games use “Frontline” differently — as a game title, game mode, or multiplayer format.


Specs, mechanics and availability vary a lot depending on which “Frontline” you meant. For example, the newer “Frontline” on Steam appears still in development; “Frontlines: Fuel of War” — though popular — has outdated multiplayer support.


If you meant “Frontline” as a mode inside a larger game (e.g. a tank game, or mobile shooter), its rules depend heavily on that game.



